│ Okay, let’s address this issue. The core     │
│ problem is that the __deepcopy__ method in   │
│ Django’s Field class performs a shallow copy │
│ of the error_messages dictionary, leading to │
│ shared error messages across form instances. │
│ This violates the expectation that each form │
│ instance should have its own, independent    │
│ set of error messages.                       │
│                                              │
│ The fix involves creating a deep copy of the │
│ error_messages dictionary within the         │
│ __deepcopy__ method. This ensures that each  │
│ copied form instance receives a completely   │
│ independent copy of the error messages,      │
│ preventing unintended side effects.          │
│                                              │
│ Here's the patch:                            │
│                                              │
│ ┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓ │
│ ┃              modification 1              ┃ │
│ ┗━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┛ │
│                                              │
│                                              │
│  [file][file]django/forms/fields.py[/file]   │
│  [original]def __deepcopy__(self, memo={}):  │
│      cls = self.__class__                    │
│      new_field = cls.__new__(cls,            │
│  self.as_dict())                             │
│      memo.add(self)                          │
│      new_field.id = self.id                  │
│      new_field.name = self.name              │
│      new_field.default_value =               │
│  self.default_value                          │
│      new_field.required = self.required      │
│      new_field.choices = self.choices        │
│      new_field.min_length = self.min_length  │
│      new_field.max_length = self.max_length  │
│      new_field.help_text = self.help_text    │
│      new_field.label = self.label            │
│      new_field.initial = self.initial        │
│      new_field.widget = self.widget          │
│      new_field.template = self.template      │
│      new_field.strip = self.strip            │
│      new_field.error_messages =              │
│  memo.setdefault(id(self), {})               │
│      return new_field                        │
│  [/original]                                 │
│  [patched]def __deepcopy__(self, memo={}):   │
│      cls = self.__class__                    │
│      new_field = cls.__new__(cls,            │
│  self.as_dict())                             │
│      memo.add(self)                          │
│      new_field.id = self.id                  │
│      new_field.name = self.name              │
│      new_field.default_value =               │
│  self.default_value                          │
│      new_field.required = self.required      │
│      new_field.choices = self.choices        │
│      new_field.min_length = self.min_length  │
│      new_field.max_length = self.max_length  │
│      new_field.help_text = self.help_text    │
│      new_field.label = self.label            │
│      new_field.initial = self.initial        │
│      new_field.widget = self.widget          │
│      new_field.template = self.template      │
│      new_field.strip = self.strip            │
│      new_field.error_messages =              │
│  copy.deepcopy(self.error_messages, memo)    │
│      return new_field                        │
│  [/patched]                                  │
│                                              │
│                                              │
│ Note: I've added import copy to the patched  │
│ code. This is necessary to use the           │
│ copy.deepcopy function.                      │
